#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216999 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61c3724b-7fda-5547-b0cb-7bf9136fc4a3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gravitational Force Between Two Objects"}] :question/id #uuid "f37e7ffd-df2d-51a4-9eee-e53589011f6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21744" :question/problem "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times10^{30})\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and Mars has a mass of $(6.42\\times10^{23})\\,\\textrm{kg}.$\nThe average distance between the centers of the Sun and Mars is $(2.28\\times10^{11})\\,\\textrm{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the Sun and Mars? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2).$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}),$\n\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}.$\n\nLet $m_1$ be the mass of the Sun and $m_2$ be the mass of Mars. Then, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\cdot (6.42\\times {10}^{23})}{(2.28\\times {10}^{11})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 1.99\\cdot 6.42}{(2.28)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}\\cdot {10}^{23}}{({10}^{11})^{2}} \\\\  & =(16.392\\,5…)\\times {10}^{20} \\\\  & =(1.639\\,25…)\\times {10}^{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the gravitational force between the Sun and Mars is\n$\\Big(1.64 \\times 10^{21}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{N}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217000 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd5da040-4fa3-5aeb-a98f-2a2ba03f5e16" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Mass of an Object"}] :question/id #uuid "2ef6ba7e-4f1d-51d2-af64-e1a77a7d748f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21745" :question/problem "The centers of two objects are separated by a distance of $(3.0\\times10^3)\\,\\textrm{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(3.0\\times10^{-4})\\,\\textrm{N}.$ If one object has mass $(2.0\\times10^5)\\,\\textrm{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of the other object? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2).$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}),$\n\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}.$\n\nLet $m_2$ be the unknown mass.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $m_2$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(3.0\\times {10}^{-4})\\cdot (3.0\\times {10}^{3})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (2.0\\times {10}^{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3.0\\cdot (3.0)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 2.0}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-4}\\cdot ({10}^{3})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{5}} \\\\  & =(2.023…)\\times {10}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the mass of the other object is\n$\\Big(2.0 \\times 10^{8}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{kg}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217001 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11810d4b-610c-5dac-8fe7-abf9391c2aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Objects"}] :question/id #uuid "e7043d87-7f25-55a3-bd36-49d4ba1ae3fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-21746" :question/problem "Earth has a mass of $(5.97\\times10^{24})\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and the Moon has a mass of $(7.35\\times10^{22})\\,\\textrm{kg}.$ The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(1.98\\times10^{20})\\,\\textrm{N}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of Earth and the Moon? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2).$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}),$\n\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $m_1$ be the mass of Earth and $m_2$ be the mass of the Moon. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\cdot (7.35\\times {10}^{22})}{1.98\\times {10}^{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 5.97\\cdot 7.35}{1.98}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{24}\\cdot {10}^{22}}{{10}^{20}} \\\\  & =(147.816…)\\times {10}^{15} \\\\  & =(14.7816…)\\times {10}^{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{14.7816…\\times {10}^{16}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14.7816…}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{16}} \\\\  & =(3.8446…)\\times {10}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between Earth and the Moon is\n$\\Big(3.84 \\times 10^{8}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61c3724b-7fda-5547-b0cb-7bf9136fc4a3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gravitational Force Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "a25b56a7-7a1c-508b-82a2-39c31264ab50" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216585} {:answer-field/id #uuid "5a806970-7574-5f6d-a0e0-0f388ffb134c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216586}] :question/id #uuid "4f08a74f-5dc1-59fb-934d-192510b18902" :question/math-academy-id "q-334944" :question/problem "The center of two objects of masses $(7.5 \\cdot 10^{5})kg$ and $(2.0 \\cdot 10^{6})kg$ are separated by a distance of $(4.0 \\cdot 10^{2})m$. According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67 \\cdot 10^{-11})m^{3}/(kg \\cdot s^{2})$.The magnitude of the force between the two objects is\n$($ {{field-1}}\n$\\cdot$\n{{field-2}} $)N$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217789 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd5da040-4fa3-5aeb-a98f-2a2ba03f5e16" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Mass of an Object"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "8411272a-b29f-5b62-85b4-24db9b8399d2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216591} {:answer-field/id #uuid "e908cf01-0b05-5721-8a0d-853b457b6c59" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216592}] :question/id #uuid "40755d2c-612a-5ccf-a761-10556a446e0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-335126" :question/problem "The average distance between the centers of Earth and the Moon is $(3.84 \\cdot 10^{8})m$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(1.98 \\cdot 10^{20})N$. Given that the mass of Earth is $(5.97 \\cdot 10^{24})kg$, then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of the Moon? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67 \\cdot 10^{-11})m^{3}/(kg \\cdot s^{2})$.The mass of the Moon is\n$($ {{field-1}}\n$\\cdot$\n{{field-2}} $)kg$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11810d4b-610c-5dac-8fe7-abf9391c2aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "fe11665f-a742-5b8a-ac1c-8d9534f7c414" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216593} {:answer-field/id #uuid "011c8996-2157-52f6-9093-6078e9a4fedc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216594}] :question/id #uuid "141575dc-d270-56a4-9e5c-9b5ce1733ede" :question/math-academy-id "q-335148" :question/problem "The gravitational force between two objects, of masses $(6.2 \\cdot 10^{6})kg$ and $(9.5 \\cdot 10^{5})kg$, has a magnitude of $(1.9 \\cdot 10^{-3})N$.\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67 \\cdot 10^{-11})m^{3}/(kg \\cdot s^{2})$.The distance between the two objects is\n$($ {{field-1}}\n$\\cdot$\n{{field-2}} $)m$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61c3724b-7fda-5547-b0cb-7bf9136fc4a3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gravitational Force Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad602a92-a4e1-55b3-a5f2-ad4a71149f37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad602a92-a4e1-55b3-a5f2-ad4a71149f37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310149} :answer-field/id #uuid "50cb604b-e0fb-5afa-b0cc-f5ebfbeca6b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311002} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ff45d5-e2a8-553c-8663-3825a63e87a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10}^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36ff45d5-e2a8-553c-8663-3825a63e87a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10}^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309166} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0ad518d-938b-547f-bb69-a628280fe3fb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fdaf854a-23e0-590a-afed-80c02813eae0" :question/math-academy-id "q-336275" :question/problem "The centers of two objects of masses $(6.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(4.2\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ are separated by a distance of $(7.5\\times {10}^{2})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(6.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(4.2\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(6.5\\times {10}^{5})\\cdot (4.2\\times {10}^{6})}{(7.5\\times {10}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 6.5\\cdot 4.2}{(7.5)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{5}\\cdot {10}^{6}}{({10}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =3.237\\,173…\\times {10}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects is\n$(\\,3.2\\times {10}^{-4}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd5da040-4fa3-5aeb-a98f-2a2ba03f5e16" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Mass of an Object"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "085a39b8-34b4-59be-bf78-3ad89c9cee72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10}^{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "085a39b8-34b4-59be-bf78-3ad89c9cee72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10}^{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308811} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f64e729-bdd6-5060-8792-c766db195183" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311089} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b78827-bc2a-5019-be53-c37837890ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01b78827-bc2a-5019-be53-c37837890ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308759} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb7af761-a937-5f8b-85e7-5f8e2b17cdfd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6fc2d2a-8149-567b-ab06-063d27b8e5bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-336295" :question/problem "The average distance between the centers of the Sun and Mars is $(2.28\\times {10}^{11})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(1.64\\times {10}^{21})\\,\\text{N}.$ Given that the mass of the Sun is $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of Mars? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of Mars is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Mars.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(1.64\\times {10}^{21})\\cdot (2.28\\times {10}^{11})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (1.99\\times {10}^{30})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.64\\cdot (2.28)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 1.99}\\times \\frac{{10}^{21}\\cdot ({10}^{11})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}} \\\\  & =(0.642\\,29…)\\times {10}^{24} \\\\  & =(6.4229…)\\times {10}^{23}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the mass of Mars is\n$(\\,6.42\\times {10}^{23}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311686 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11810d4b-610c-5dac-8fe7-abf9391c2aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcbb534d-2c92-5c2f-a8fe-e17d3ad2fd0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcbb534d-2c92-5c2f-a8fe-e17d3ad2fd0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310278} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c8396d6-798a-55e6-bbe8-0d0f07b1c64a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311034} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de2407ae-b970-55d0-82cd-097b26f2d840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10}^{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de2407ae-b970-55d0-82cd-097b26f2d840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10}^{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310529} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6f47661-d725-5de6-a866-704c55c0d262" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4734863f-a900-55bb-995a-471d13e8ef2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-336307" :question/problem "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Neptune has a mass of $(1.02\\times {10}^{26})\\,\\text{kg}.$ The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(6.69\\times {10}^{20})\\,\\text{N}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the Sun and Neptune? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the Sun and Neptune is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Neptune. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\cdot (1.02\\times {10}^{26})}{6.69\\times {10}^{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 1.99\\cdot 1.02}{6.69}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}\\cdot {10}^{26}}{{10}^{20}} \\\\  & =(2.0237…)\\times {10}^{25} \\\\  & =(20.237…)\\times {10}^{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{20.237…\\times {10}^{24}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20.237…}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{24}} \\\\  & =(4.498…)\\times {10}^{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between the Sun and Neptune is\n$(\\,4.50\\times {10}^{12}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11810d4b-610c-5dac-8fe7-abf9391c2aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0658bcd-109d-5074-ae2f-ffd4d820d520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10}^{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0658bcd-109d-5074-ae2f-ffd4d820d520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10}^{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310172} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e72599a-c501-53ec-bc0d-dc66e0a2c2cb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310961} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97659834-b505-529e-8bad-43eb98c2047b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97659834-b505-529e-8bad-43eb98c2047b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309961} :answer-field/id #uuid "92734986-b058-5cad-a184-d151f6495698" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ad559c9-9cfd-5c87-86a0-09ca886a809d" :question/math-academy-id "q-336314" :question/problem "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Venus has a mass of $(4.87\\times {10}^{24})\\,\\text{kg}.$ The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(5.54\\times {10}^{22})\\,\\text{N}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the Sun and Venus? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the Sun and Venus is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Venus. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\cdot (4.87\\times {10}^{24})}{5.54\\times {10}^{22}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 1.99\\cdot 4.87}{5.54}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}\\cdot {10}^{24}}{{10}^{22}} \\\\  & =(11.668…)\\times {10}^{21} \\\\  & =(1.1668…)\\times {10}^{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{1.1668…\\times {10}^{22}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1.1668…}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{22}} \\\\  & =(1.0801…)\\times {10}^{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between the Sun and Venus is\n$(\\,1.08\\times {10}^{11}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$"}]}
